    "turn_text": "And so then what was your first memory? So when you were sort of growing up with all the things she was doing, what were some of your early memories of her and the struggle to help the community? And, you know, the thing about your mind, I remember even during COVID, I mean, I have a lot of memories of her, right? Even during COVID, I went to Booker T to get my shot. And there she was. I mean, like a general, making sure there was enough COVID vaccines, and it was all going well. And she was marching up and down to make sure that the community was being protected. There she was. It's just amazing. And I remember, because everybody knows I ran for mayor, and everybody knows your mom ran for mayor. And we were so friendly on the campaign trail, because early on, she told me, go, we remember. You know, we may be opponents in this race, but you know, my enemy. And we need a lot more of that. And I remember, and she may not have said it openly, but whenever there were debates, because we had a lot of debates and forums, she always wanted to be by me. I mean, because we got along great, and sometimes we would share notes about things, you know. It was so much fun to be with her. And I remember a lot of people told me, How come you always say such nice things about her? uh... and i said well because she earned it and what i mean by that is i was always uh... taking shots at uh... john wittmeyer because i was concerned he would not commit that he would not touch the metro money because that's got to be firm for rail and and for transit uh... and i would then leave that into because it's real that we would not have gotten that one billion dollar grant from the federal government without your mom No way. No way. And, um, and I remember she was just, you know, unrelenting on secretary of the hood. I mean, unrelenting, there are times when I was there where she's calling him and she would talk to him like, and we got to get that money. I mean, there was none of this. Yes, sir. Mr. Secretary. I mean, she was just, you know, damn. and we got the money. And what was more important about it is during that time, remember the new board came in, Mayor Parker came in and the previous board had all kinds of You know, lack of transparency, financial issues, we can go on and on. I'm not trying to build up ancient history. But I remember the administrator, who's like the one that signs the check for the Department of Transportation. So not the secretary himself, but the administrator came down and we had a meeting and it was me and the president, which was at the time it was George Grenius. And then it was your mom, I'm trying to think who else was there, Jean Green, Mayor Parker, all the delegation. And he came down and said, well, you're not gonna get the billion dollar grant because the previous board had violated their own procurement laws. and I mean my heart sank because we had already dug up the streets.",
